  "code": "internal",
  "intro": {
   "problem": "Network architectures in physics analyses are usually chosen by convention, and little evidence says the default topology is right once a network must deliver background closure [@abcdisco2020][@cms2025tec] as well as discrimination.",
   "value": "A statistically scored topology search, borrowed from generative structural design [@hahn2023][@nie2020], could find networks that keep their discriminating power while closing better, replacing architecture-by-habit with architecture-by-evidence.",
   "approach": "TRUSS has two components. Testing existing: measure how much adding or removing connections moves the current networks, then relax that grid into learned GRU-style sigmoid gates on connection groups [@cho2014][@louizos2018]. Testing new: generate candidates from the trained models' latent features using gated blocks [@dauphin2017][@srivastava2015], growth [@evci2022] and evolution [@real2018]. Every candidate is trained supervised, and a surrogate fitted to those results makes the search itself supervised [@nie2020].",
   "goals": "The deliverables are a calibrated connection sensitivity, a population of tested architectures retrained from scratch on seeds disjoint from the search [@zela2019], a random-search baseline beside every search, and a Pareto front of the real trade-offs."
  },
